{
  "biological_interpretation": "Acute neuronal loss of TDP-43 or FUS is sufficient to create strong RNA-processing abundance states in selected thalamic, midbrain, and GABAergic cells, but the response is not a generic consequence of ALS/FTD gene perturbation and does not reproduce every canonical human cryptic-exon scalar marker.",
  "caveats": [
    "The output is 3-prime mRNA abundance and cannot directly observe cryptic-exon inclusion.",
    "Several negative ALS comparators were transcriptionally weak, so they reject phenocopy of the observed acute state rather than every protein-level disease mechanism."
  ],
  "cell_types": [
    "151 TH Prkcd Grin2c Glut",
    "155 MB Glut",
    "191 MB P MY GABA",
    "052 Pvalb Gaba"
  ],
  "exact_observed_result": "Tardbp depletion repeatedly induced Ppp6c/Fam135a/Usp15/Gabpa while repressing Snrnp70 in 151 TH, 155 MB, and 191 MB classes; Fus depletion induced Rsrp1 and Atxn2l with Luc7l2/Snrnp70 movement in 151 TH and midbrain classes, while Grn, Nek1, Setx, and Tbk1 did not phenocopy these RNA-binding protein states.",
